+    <details>
+      <summary>Priority vs Weighted classes &mdash; when to use which?</summary>
+      <div class="fc">
+        <b>Priority</b> (the default) is absolute rank: under load, a higher class takes everything its ceiling allows before a lower class gets a byte. Perfect for latency tiers.<br><br>
+        <b>Weighted</b> classes opt out of ranking: all weighted classes form one pool <i>below</i> every Priority class, and the pool splits whatever bandwidth is left in proportion to weight (1&ndash;63). Use it when two bulk-ish classes should share fairly instead of one starving the other &mdash; e.g. make Downloads (weight 40) and a Backups class (weight 20) weighted, and backups always get a third of the leftover no matter how greedy downloads are.<br><br>
+        Rule the editor enforces: weighted rows sit together at the bottom &mdash; a Priority row below the pool would silently outrank the whole pool.
+      </div>
+    </details>
+    <details>
+      <summary>Can I cap a GROUP of classes together?</summary>
+      <div class="fc">There is no group-shaper control (the hardware's shaper-object API is not usable from the firmware's tooling), but the same outcome falls out of two existing levers: with the <b>aggregate cap on</b>, give the group members ceilings that <b>sum to the group budget</b> &mdash; e.g. Streaming 30% + Downloads 30% can never exceed 60% combined. Add weights (previous question) if the members should also share that budget fairly.</div>
+    </details>
+    <details>
+      <summary>What is L4S and should I turn it on?</summary>
+      <div class="fc">L4S (Low Latency, Low Loss, Scalable throughput) is a newer congestion-control standard: capable flows get their congestion signaled by <b>marking</b> packets (ECN) instead of dropping them, so the sender backs off within a round-trip and queueing stays near zero. Newer Apple devices and some conferencing/game stacks negotiate it; everything else is unaffected and keeps normal PI2 behavior.<br><br>It is marked <b>experimental</b> here: the queue hardware advertises the capability but it has not been proven on this board. Safe to try &mdash; if it misbehaves (a class suddenly shows zero drops but poor latency), switch it back off and re-apply.</div>
+    </details>
     <details>
       <summary>Why do bulk classes get a HIGH ceiling?</summary>
       <div class="fc">Strict priority already makes the bottom class yield the moment anything above it wants the line. A high ceiling (95%) simply lets backups saturate an otherwise idle link. Low priority + high ceiling is the correct combination for bulk; a low ceiling would only slow it down at 3 a.m. for no one's benefit.</div>
